  "title": "Canadian Postal Code Format Explained",
  "description": "A Canadian postal code is letter-number-letter, then number-letter-number. What each part means, what the first letter shows, and why it is not a ZIP code.",
  "keywords": [
    "canadian postal code format",
    "canadian zip code format",
    "canadian zip codes",
    "canada post postal code format"
  ],
  "answer": "A Canadian postal code has six characters in the pattern letter, number, letter, space, number, letter, number. The first three, the forward sortation area, identify a major geographic area. The last three identify the smallest delivery unit. The first letter shows the region, so M means Metropolitan Toronto. It is called a postal code, not a ZIP code.",
  "sections": [
    {
      "h2": "The structure",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "p": "Canada Post describes a postal code as a six-character alphanumeric code in the form ANA NAN, where A is a letter and N is a digit. It is written in two groups of three."
        },
        {
          "table": {
            "headers": [
              "Part",
              "Pattern",
              "What it identifies"
            ],
            "rows": [
              [
                "Forward sortation area (FSA)",
                "Letter, number, letter",
                "A major geographic area"
              ],
              [
                "Local delivery unit (LDU)",
                "Number, letter, number",
                "The smallest delivery unit within the FSA"
              ]
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "p": "The second character of the FSA is a digit from 1 to 9 for urban areas and 0 (zero) for rural ones."
        }
      ]
    },
